{"answerArea":{"calculator":true,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"We are asked to find the *second* derivative of a parametric function.\n\nRecall that the *first* derivative of a function defined parametrically by the equations $x=u(t)$ and $y=v(t)$ is found with the following rule:\n\n$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\right)}=\\dfrac{v'(t)}{u'(t)}$\n\nThen, the *second* derivative is found with this following rule:\n\n$\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{d}{dt}\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\right)}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{d}{dt}\\left(\\dfrac{v'(t)}{u'(t)}\\right)}{u'(t)}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"Let's start by finding $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$.\n\n$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=3\\cdot4^t$\n\n[[☃ explanation 1]]","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{"explanation 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"explanation":"We are given that $x=2^t$ and $y=8^t$:\n\n$\\begin{align}\n\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\u0026=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{d}{dt}(8^t)}{\\dfrac{d}{dt}(2^t)}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{8^t\\ln(8)}{2^t\\ln(2)}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=4^t\\cdot\\dfrac{\\ln(2^3)}{\\ln(2)}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=4^t\\cdot\\dfrac{3\\ln(2)}{\\ln(2)}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=4^t\\cdot3\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=3\\cdot4^t\n\\end{align}$","hidePrompt":"Got it, thanks!","showPrompt":"Show entire calculation.","static":false,"widgets":{}},"static":false,"type":"explanation","version":{"major":0,"minor":0}}}},{"content":"Now we can find $\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}$.\n\n$\\begin{align}\n\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}\u0026=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{d}{dt}\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\right)}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{d}{dt}\\left(3\\cdot4^t\\right)}{\\dfrac{d}{dt}(2^t)}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{3\\cdot4^t\\ln(4)}{2^t\\ln(2)}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=3\\cdot2^t\\cdot\\dfrac{\\ln(2^2)}{\\ln(2)}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=3\\cdot2^t\\cdot\\dfrac{2\\ln(2)}{\\ln(2)}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=6\\cdot2^t\n\\end{align}$\n","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"In conclusion, $\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}=6\\cdot2^t$.","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=2^t$ and $y=8^t$.\n\n**What is $\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}$ in terms of $t$ ?**\n\n[[☃ radio 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"radio 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"choices":[{"content":"$6\\cdot2^t$","correct":true},{"content":"$3\\cdot4^t$","correct":false},{"content":"$\\dfrac{3\\cdot2^t}{\\ln(2)}$","correct":false,"isNoneOfTheAbove":false},{"content":"$4^t$","correct":false,"isNoneOfTheAbove":false}],"deselectEnabled":false,"displayCount":null,"hasNoneOfTheAbove":false,"multipleSelect":false,"onePerLine":false,"randomize":true},"static":false,"type":"radio","version":{"major":1,"minor":0}}}}}
